contrib/pg_bp_clir/README

pg_bp_clir -- Clock-LIRS: lock-light scan-resistant replacement
================================================================

pg_bp_clir provides CLIR, a clock-style realisation of the LIRS family
of buffer replacement algorithms.  It targets the same workloads as
pg_bp_lirs (and pg_bp_rcrd / LIRS2) -- mixed OLTP + scan, working sets
that exceed cache, scan resistance for sequential reads -- but uses
single-word atomic operations on the hot path instead of the spinlock-
protected linked-list manipulation that contended in plain LIRS.

The transformation from LIRS to CLIR is the same one Bansal & Modha
applied to ARC to produce CAR (FAST 2004): replace the recency-ordered
list with a circular array of slots and per-slot reference bits, drive
victim selection by clock-walk + second-chance, and keep the ghost-
queue logic only on the cold (miss) path.  The result is much more
parallelism-friendly under high concurrency on multi-core hardware.

The ghost-queue + adaptation step is taken under a fine-grained
adapt_lock that is acquired only by on_miss / on_evict / on_new_tag.
The hot-path on_hit is one pg_atomic_fetch_or_u32 to set the
reference bit on the slot the buffer owns -- no locks, no list walk,
no allocation.

scan_resistant
==============

CLIR sets BufferPoolRoutine.scan_resistant = true.  The scan-resistance
property carries over from LIRS: a sequential scan of pages with no
prior reference cycles through the HIR clock, which is sized at ~1%
of the pool (min 32 slots).  LIR-tier pages are protected; their slots
get their reference bits cleared as the LIR clock hand sweeps but they
are never selected as victims while there are HIR slots to evict.
This is the property the framework's GetAccessStrategyWithSize() uses
to skip the RECYCLE-pool detour for BUF_INTENT_BULKREAD scans on relations
backed by a CLIR pool.

Installation
============

    shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_bp_clir'
    buffer_pool_algorithm    = 'clir'   # to use CLIR for the DEFAULT pool

Or per-relation, after CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_clir:

    CREATE BUFFER POOL my_clir_pool
        HANDLER clir_pool_handler SIZE '256MB';
    CREATE TABLE big_table (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_clir_pool');

When to use CLIR
================

  - High-concurrency OLTP workloads (many backends pinning / unpinning
    pages per second) where pg_bp_lirs's spinlock would bottleneck.
  - Scan-heavy mixed workloads where you need the LIRS scan-resistance
    proof without the per-hit list-manipulation overhead.

When not to use CLIR
====================

  - Single-backend workloads where ARC-on-LRU-lists has near-zero
    contention.  In that scenario plain pg_bp_lirs or pg_bp_arc may
    win on hit ratio because their access tracking has finer
    resolution than CLIR's reference-bit-or-not.
  - Workloads with very small working sets (< 1MB) where the clock
    walk's cache-line traffic dominates and per-pool clock-sweep
    is fine.

How it works (brief)
====================

CLIR maintains two clocks plus a ghost FIFO:

    lir_clock[lir_size]    LIR (low IRR / hot) pages, ~99% of pool
    hir_clock[hir_size]    HIR (high IRR / cold) pages, ~1% of pool
    ghost[ghost_size]      bounded FIFO of recently-evicted page tags

A side table maps buf_id -> (which clock, which slot) for O(1)
on_hit:

    on_hit(buf_id):
        ref = per_buffer[buf_id - first];
        atomic_or(&clock[ref.clock][ref.pos].ref_state, REF_BIT);

Victim selection walks hir_clock from hir_hand: slots with the ref
bit set get it cleared (second-chance) and the hand advances; the
first slot with the ref bit clear is the victim.  Falls back to
lir_clock only when hir_clock is empty.

On miss + ghost-list hit: the page was previously LIR.  Reinstate as
LIR and demote some LIR slot to HIR (LIRS adaptation step).

On miss + no ghost hit: insert as HIR.  A subsequent re-reference
within the ghost-FIFO horizon promotes it to LIR via the path above.

References
==========

  - S. Jiang, F. Chen, X. Zhang, "CLOCK-Pro: An Effective Improvement
    of the CLOCK Replacement", USENIX ATC 2005.
  - S. Jiang, X. Zhang, "LIRS: An Efficient Low Inter-reference
    Recency Set Replacement Policy", SIGMETRICS 2002.
  - S. Bansal, D. Modha, "CAR: Clock with Adaptive Replacement",
    USENIX FAST 2004.
  - https://github.com/ben-manes/caffeine/wiki/Efficiency
